<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#deportation">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Deportation</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as mass deportation</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Deportation refers to the forced removal of people from their homes to other places. Mass deportation was a systematic tool of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire</a>, used to break resistance and redistribute labour, most famously in the Babylonian exile of the Judeans. It was also practised by the Egyptian Empire <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/deportation.jpg" alt="Deportation" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles-Michel Geoffroy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prisoners_and_gendarms_on_the_road_to_Siberia_(Geoffroy,_1845).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Deportation is the expulsion of a person or group by a state from its sovereign territory. Its definition varies with place, context and period. Some definitions require transfer beyond the state's borders, which distinguishes it from forcible transfer inside them. Collective expulsion, which compels non-nationals as a group to leave without a reasonable and objective examination of each case, is prohibited by several instruments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Expulsions are well recorded in antiquity, particularly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>. The kingdoms of Israel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> suffered several, among them deportations by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neo-assyrian-empire">Neo-Assyrian Empire</a> after the fall of Israel and during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sennacherib">Sennacherib</a>'s campaign in the 8th century BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a> deported much of the Judean population when it conquered Judah in 597 and 587 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> used deportation against rebellious peoples, with no ill-treatment recorded.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a> used it widely in their wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. Romans defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-edessa">Battle of Edessa</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shapur-i">Shapur I</a>, including the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valerian">Valerian</a>, were taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a> and other regions, and cities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishapur">Bishapur</a> and Gundeshapur were populated by prisoners of war, who were also given farmland. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the Almoravid state deported Christians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> in 1109, 1126, 1130 and 1138.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-discovery">Age of Discovery</a>, deporting individuals to overseas colonies became common. From 1717 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> deported around 40,000 religious objectors and criminals to America until 1776, and then sent more than 160,000 convicts to the Australian colonies between 1787 and 1855. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, the Grand Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> developed internal exile, beginning with the transfers that followed its conquest of the Novgorod Republic in the 15th century, and the practice continued as katorga and later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulag">Gulag</a>.</p><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a> in the 1930s and 1940s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> carried out forced transfers of some 6 million people, with millions of deaths, and many historians have described them as ethnic cleansing, crimes against humanity or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> deported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a> and homosexuals to concentration and extermination camps, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a> the word deportation was a euphemism that frequently meant the victims were then killed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Deportation” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The empire's systematic <b>deportation</b> policy moved whole communities from conquered territories to the Assyrian heartland and to under-populated provinces, which broke potential resistance and supplied agricultural and craft labour where the state needed it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Saxon Wars (772–804) subjected the pagan Saxons east of the Rhine through more than thirty years of brutal campaigning, <b>mass deportations</b> and forced Christianisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>deportation</b> policy, most famously applied to Judah in 597 and 587/586 BCE, had an economic as well as a political purpose: craftsmen, administrators and labourers were resettled in Babylonia to supply the enormous building programmes under way there.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chaldean-empire">Chaldean Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>deportations</b> of 1864 created a large Circassian diaspora that lives today in Turkey, Jordan, Syria, Israel and other countries, and its memory remains central to Circassian identity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/circassia">Circassia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Polish Army was then disbanded, and Poles faced confiscation of property, <b>deportation</b>, forced military service and the closing of their universities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/congress-poland">Congress Poland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The khanate is the historical homeland state of the Crimean Tatars, whose national identity, language and memory of statehood remain tied to it, especially after their <b>mass deportation</b> by Stalin’s government in 1944 and their return from the late 1980s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
